This file documents the functions specific to the default template.


=== General ===

The status variables are used as follows:

* A = Ammunition. If shooting is allowed then the player can shoot bullets
by pushing shift and arrows; each shot uses up one point of ammunition.

* C = Money. It does not do anything by itself, but you may have scripts
that accept money in exchange for other stuff.

* D = Keys. Each of the low 8-bits means one colour of keys.

* E = Energy. While this is positive, the player is flashy and cannot be
damaged; it will be decreased by one each turn.

* G = Gems. Like money, it does not do anything by itself.

* H = Health. Normally you start with 100 health (you can change this in
the status variables menu in the editor), and stuff that damages the player
will take ten healths; once it reaches zero, you are dead.

* S = Score. Does nothing by itself, but some things will increase the
score. You can also adjust score by the #GIVE command in a script.

* T = Torches. In a dark room, push T to light the torch, which has a
limited duration; this uses up one torch.

* W = Light. This is used when a torch is lit to determine the duration of
the torch. You should not use #GIVE and #TAKE with this variable.

* X = Time. In a board that has a time limit, this is reset to the board's
time limit when entering the board and when damaged; if it reaches zero,
then the player is damaged.

* Z = Stone. Like money, it does not do anything by itself.

The first two stats on each board have a predefined meaning. Stat 1 is the
player, and should always have exactly one instance. When you add a new
board, you should always add the player into the new board, too. Stat 2 is
used for projectiles, and should always be present even if there are no
projectiles on that board.

The board info has some additional options:

* Restart if zapped: If selected, then whenever the player is damaged, the
player is automatically teleported to the position where the player has
entered this board. (This is independent of persistence, and this will not
reset the board even if it is not persistent; a board is only reset if you
leave and then enter a non-persistent board again.)

* Time limit: If nonzero, then it sets a time limit; see the description of
the "X" status variable above. Normally if this is set, you would also set
"restart if zapped", so that you will go back to the beginning if damaged,
but this is not mandatory.

* Max shots: The maximum number of player bullets that can be on the board
at once; if attempting to shoot when there are more than this many, then
the player cannot shoot. If this is set to zero, then the player is not
allowed to shoot at all on this board.

The default overlay is filled but nor normally visible; if the overlay is
visible then the room is dark, and a torch can be lit to temporarily allow
a part of the area around the torch to be visible.


=== Elements (F1) ===

Items:

Gem
  Can be collected. Provides one gem and ten points. Gems (including magic
  gems) can also be broken by player bullets and by crushing.

Magic Gem
  Like the gem, but also gives one health as well.

Money
  Can be collected. Each coin is worth one piece of money.

Ammo
  Can be collected. Each one gives you 5 points of ammunition.

Torch
  Can be collected and used in the underground for light.

Heart
  Gives five healths when collected.

Key
  Keys can be used to unlock and open doors of the same colour. You can
  only carry up to one key of each colour at once. Bright and dark colours
  are considered equivalent for this purpose; e.g. colour $04 and $0C are
  both considered "red". Background colours are also ignored.

Door
  Can be unlocked by a key; the key is then used up. The background colour
  of the door determines which key opens it, but if default colours are
  enabled then it will automatically change your selected colour so that
  the foreground will now be the background colour.

Stone
  Gives one stone (no inherent meaning). The colours and appearance (which
  are English alphabets) will change at random at run time.

Energizer
  Sets your energy to 75 (any energy you already have is wasted), making
  you temporarily invulnerable to damage.

Potion
  Potions have various possible effects:
  * Dud: No effect.
  * Healing: +10 health.
  * Poison: -10 health (this does not count as damage).
  * Energy: +70 energy.
  * Reveal Walls: Makes invisible walls visible.
  * Extra Healing: +50 health.
  * Time: +25 time (should only be used if there is a time limit).
  * Avalanche: Adds rocks into the board at random positions.
  * Destroy Creatures: Destroys creatures.
  * Explode Bombs: Explosives are triggered immediately.

Scroll
  Contains a script, which will be displayed or executed when the scroll is
  collected. Each scroll can only be used once.

Checkpoint
  Useful when the board is set for restart when zapped; in this case, when
  the player is damaged, you are moved to where the checkpoint was at,
  instead of back to the start.

Chest
  May contain something; once it is used, then the chest will be empty. The
  contents of the chest can be ammo, money, gems, stones, torches, or trap.
  Ammo, money, and gems will be five times the number specified, and a trap
  will damage the player for ten points of damage.

Pouch
  Contains money and/or gems. (The numbers specified are multiplied by 5;
  the pouch may contain up to 75 gems and/or 75 money.)

Explosives:

Bomb
  When touched, it will count down and then explode after a delay; the
  explosion will destroy breakable walls, creatures, and some other things.

Lit Bomb
  A bomb that is already activated and will explode soon.


=== Elements (F2) ===

Creatures:
  Most of these creatures have parameters that can be set, such as
  intelligence, which determines how likely the creature is to move
  toward the player. Most creatures will damage the player and themself
  if they touch the player, and can be destroyed by the player's bullets
  and by explosives, many can also be crushed by boulders, etc.

Lion
  A simple creature which moves according to its intelligence parameter.

Tiger
  Like the lion but can also shoot bullets or stars. The firing rate
  specifies how likely it is to shoot.

Bear
  Always moves directly toward the player if the player is in range,
  otherwise stands still. If it moves into a breakable wall, both the
  wall and the bear are destroyed.

Shark
  Also works like a lion, but moves only on water, and cannot move on land.
  Unlike ZZT, sharks can be destroyed by bullets and other methods.

Runner
  Moves in one direction, and may push things out of the way. If it cannot
  move, it reverses direction.

Slime
  Gradually spreads over time, filling up space with breakable walls. If
  slime is touched by the player, it stops spreading at that point, but
  any other slime that is still spreading will continue. Slime will not
  damage the player, but may make the player get stuck.

Mouse
  Like a lion but tries to move away from the player instead of toward.

Snake
  Moves as far as it can in one direction; once it is blocked, then it
  will try to change direction to another way and continue moving.

Spider
  Moves only on webs.

Bird
  Is able to fly over water as well as on empty spaces.

Lumberjack
  Moves (like a lion and other creatures do), but if there is a forest or a
  tree in the way, will destroy it to allow the lumberjack to pass.

Dragon
  A dragon will move and will spit fire. Touching a dragon will not harm
  the dragon but does harm the player. Bullets and explosives can harm a
  dragon, but must be damaged multiple times (according to the number of
  its hit points) before it is destroyed. It normally always tries to go
  directly toward the player, but you can select random movement one eighth
  of the time.

Centipedes:
  A centipede is made up of the head and several segments; place the head
  first, and then place the segments leading from the head, which should be
  adjacent to form a path. The entire centipede then moves together. If one
  piece of the centipede is broken, then it will split and the part of the
  tail near the split point will change to another head, making it into two
  centipedes instead of only one. Note that the intelligence and deviance
  parameters are centipedes go up to 128, rather than 15 like others do.


=== Elements (F3) ===

Terrains:

Water
  Blocks the movement of the player and most other things, but some things
  are not blocked by water, such as bullets, stars, and sharks.

Forest
  Blocks movement of most things, but the player can go through it, and
  clearing the path for other things to go through, when doing so.

Ice
  If the player steps on the ice, then the player will continue moving in
  the same direction until stopped or if you try to move a different way.

Web
  Does not block the player, but spiders are confined to moving on webs.

Tree
  Blocks movement of most things, like a wall. However, trees can be
  destroyed by fire and by lumberjacks.

Fire
  It is hot. The player can step on it but is damaged by doing so.

Walls:
  Most of these walls block movement of everything.

Solid
  Blocks movement of everything and cannot be destroyed.

Normal
  Blocks movement of everything and cannot be destroyed.

Line
  Like a solid or normal wall, but the lines will automatically be joined
  together when placed on the board. If they are added or removed at run
  time, the lines will also automatically be corrected.

Breakable
  Blocks movement, but can be destroyed by bullets, stars, explosives, etc.

Text
  Like a solid wall but any character code can be used. You can also push
  F6 to enter text directly.

Invisible
  This wall is invisible, but will become visible when touched.

Floors:

Empty
  An empty space; most things can move through it.

Floor
  Most things can move through it, like an empty space, but the floor is
  visible and can be of a specific colour.

Fake
  Also a kind of floor, but appears to be a normal wall. However, you and
  other stuff can still move through it since it is not a wall.

Still/flowing water:
  These are not the same as the Water element. The player and many other
  things can move on them, that cannot move on Water. When the player is
  standing in flowing water, the player will be moved.


=== Elements (F4) ===

Puzzles:

Rock
  Can be pushed by the player, but cannot be pushed indirectly. If it is
  pushed into water, it gets rid of both the rock and the water, allowing
  the player to pass.

Boulder
  Can be pushed directly by the player or indirectly by others, and it is
  also possible for multiples to be pushed at once.

Slider N/S
  Like the boulder but only goes in the direction of the arrows.

Slider E/W
  Like the boulder but only goes in the direction of the arrows.

Pusher
  This automatically moves in the direction it is pointing, pushing things
  along its path.

Indirect Push
  This cannot be pushed directly by the player, but can be moved by other
  things that are able to push it.

Miscellaneous:

Transporter
  Allows movement only in the direction it is facing. If the other side is
  not blocked (after pushing stuff out of the way, if any), then whatever
  is moving through it will go through to the other side. If the other side
  is blocked, then it will find the next transporter in its path that is
  facing the opposite direction and that is not blocked, and then will be
  transported to that one. (This is like the transporter in ZZT.)

Ricochet
  A wall that will reflect bullets that strike it, the opposite way.

Spike
  A wall that hurts the player if touched.

Gate
  Opens for a brief time when touched, allowing passing through.

One Step
  Can only be walked on once; after that, it will be a wall.

Land Mine
  If touched, hurts the player. The land mine only works once; after that,
  it is destroyed.

Duplicator
  Periodically duplicates whatever is in the source direction into the
  opposite direction, moving things out of the way to make room. If there
  is no room (or whatever is there cannot be moved out of the way), then
  the duplicator will not work until it is no longer blocked.

Passage
  Allows the player to go to another board. If there is another passage of
  the same colour on the target board, then the player will be at the
  location of that passage of the matching colour.

Conveyor
  Spins clockwise or counterclockwise; pushable things that are next to
  it (even diagonally) will be moved in the appropriate direction.


=== Elements (F5) ===

Guns:

Gun
  Emits bullets or stars. All parameters other than direction and spinning
  are for the stat; all guns of the same stat share those parameters. When
  adding a gun from the F5 menu, a new stat is created; using y and p to
  make copies will all share the same stat.

Laser Gun
  Emits beams. It will activate the beam, damaging some things that it can
  hit (such as the player), and then deactivate the beam at a later time;
  it will periodically switch on and off. The initial delay can be set so
  that groups of laser guns can make a kind of "wave".

Projectiles/Beams:

Bullet
  Moves in one direction until it hits something and then it disappears
  and may damage whatever it hits.

Star
  Chases the player, damaging the player (and being destroyed in the
  process) if it strikes the player. However, a star has a limited
  duration; once it expires then it disappears.

Spit Fire
  Moving fire, which will stop and become ordinary fire once it hits
  something. Trees will be burned by this fire.

Beam
  A laser beam; acts like a solid wall except if the beam hits something as
  it is being created in which case it can damage it.

Special:

Empty
  Same as in the F3 menu.

Player
  The player. There should be exactly one player per board.

Object
  A solid object, that you can specify an appearance and a script to
  define its behaviour. An object's script is executed during every
  turn, and can run asynchronously; use #END to stop execution. The
  object can receive messages and then next time it is its turn it
  will run the script if it does anything with that message. If you
  already added an object and use y and p to add more, they will be
  additional instances of the same stat and share the script, so
  modifying the script will affect all instances.

Sensor
  The player can step onto a sensor, and other things can push it. A
  sensor's script is executed when the player steps on it.


=== Script messages ===

:BOMBED
  Sent to an object when it is caught in the area of an explosion.

:ENTER
  Sent to all objects when entering a board.

:HINT
  Sent to all objects when H is pushed.

:SHOT
  Sent to an object when a bullet hits it.

:THUD
  When an object is walking and is blocked, then it receives this message.

:TOUCH
  Sent to an object when the player touches it.


=== Script commands ===

#CHAR <number>
  Changes the object's display character to the specified number. (This is
  the same as #PARAMETER but with a different name.)

#SHOOT <direction>
  Shoot a bullet in the specified direction; uses up the object's turn.

#WAIT <number>
  Wait for a specified number of frames before continuing.

#WAITFOR <condition>
  Wait until the specified condition is true before continuing. Example:
    #WAITFOR !@GEM
  This will wait until all gems are collected and then continue.

#THROWSTAR <direction> [<duration>]
  Throw a star in the specified direction. You can also specify the
  duration of the star, from 1 to 255; if not specified then the default
  duration is 127.

